17)What is a Pod and how is it different from a container?
A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It acts as a wrapper around one or more containers that run together on the same node and share:
Network (same IP & port space)
Storage volumes
Lifecycle
In Kubernetes, you don’t deploy containers directly — you deploy Pods.

18)| Feature    | **Container**                                         | **Pod**                                                               |
| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Definition | A single application package with code + dependencies | A Kubernetes object that runs one or more containers as a single unit |
| Management | Managed by container runtime (Docker, containerd)     | Managed by Kubernetes                                                 |
| Networking | Standalone container has its own network namespace    | Containers inside a Pod share the same IP, ports, and localhost       |
| Storage    | Container has its own filesystem                      | Pod can have shared volumes for data sharing among containers         |
| Use Case   | One process or microservice                           | Group of tightly coupled containers that must run together            |
| Deployment | Cannot be directly deployed in Kubernetes             | Kubernetes deploys Pods, not standalone containers                    |

